title: Daemon data in panels
tags: [[Writing a panel type]]
kind: rationale
audience: dev
aliases: gui-cache change-feed changes.ts reads.ts no-cache
summary: The GUI keeps no metarecord, path or query result: each read is a daemon round-trip, and one shared change feed tells panels when to read again — a cache was built, and removed for its bugs.

Nothing on the GUI side keeps daemon ''data'' — metarecords, paths, query results.
Each read (`metafolder.daemon.query`, `metarecords`, `treePaths`, `fields`, `call`)
is a round-trip to the daemon, which answers from its store in about a millisecond:
a page of a query ≈ 1 ms, the paths of a page ≈ 0.5 ms, one metarecord ≈ 0.15 ms on
a repository of 20 000 files. A panel keeps what it displays, for as long as it
displays it, and reads it again when told that it changed
(`frontend/src/lib/panels/reads.ts`).

!! The cache that was removed

Until September 2026 a cache shared by every panel (metarecords, tree paths, query
results) served those reads, invalidated from the change feed. It was removed once
the daemon had made it pointless, and for what it cost. Every one of its bugs came
from the same question — when does a kept answer stop being true? — answered wrong
each time:

* A change ''anywhere'' in the repository, landing while a read was in flight, threw that read away. The rows it was for kept their placeholders — a `mfr_path:path` column showing bare file names — until something else repainted them.
* A daemon ''read'' sent as a `POST` (`query/fields/resolve-tree`, `tree/resolve-path`, `orphans/scan`: the body carries the query) was taken for a write and invalidated everything. With the previous point, opening a folder in the file manager marked healthy rows of the list as orphans.
* A path spells the name of every ancestor, but renaming a directory writes that directory alone. The feed never named the files below it, and their kept paths stayed wrong until evicted.

Do not bring one back for query results or metarecords. ''Configuration is another
matter'', and is kept: it changes when a file is edited, not at every write. The
user schema is read once per repository and shared by every panel (`loadSchema`,
`/__schema-template.js`; a failed read is not kept), and an instance keeps the
answer of `GET /repos` for `repoRoot`.

!! The change feed

What remains shared is the change feed (`changes.ts`, one for the whole page). It
holds one number per repository — the head of the log at its last poll — and asks
the daemon `GET /repos/:repo/log/since?op=<head>` ([[Log endpoints]]):

* in the background, every 7 seconds, for each repository a panel has read;
* on demand, with `metafolder.changes.sync(repo)` — after the GUI changed the disk itself, once the watcher's quiet period has passed (`createCatchup`, [[Event batching]]).

Subscribers receive `{repo, uuids}`: the metarecords touched, or `null` for the
whole repository — a rollback, a list the daemon truncated, a repository that was
empty at the previous poll. The feed reports every change, a panel's own writes
included; it is the only way a panel hears of the others: the watcher recording a
rename, another panel, `mf`.

''The baseline is taken before the first read.'' The first poll of a repository
reports nothing, it only records the head. Every daemon call naming a repository
therefore waits for that first poll before it is sent. Taken after the read, the
baseline would already include whatever changed in between, and the panel would
never hear of it.

!! What a panel does with it

* It reads again what it shows, when a change names a metarecord it shows or names the whole repository.
* A panel that shows ''paths'' reads them again at ''any'' change of its repository — the directory rename above: the feed names the directory, the screen shows its contents. That is one `treePaths` call for the rows loaded.
* A read that has not landed, or failed, is ''unknown'', never ''absent''. A path not read yet says nothing about where the file is; reading it as "no path" is how a healthy row was shown as an orphaned metarecord.
* It unsubscribes in the function `mount` returns.
